```java
public final class Order {
    private final List<String> items;
    public Order(List<String> items) { this.items = items; } // stores caller's list
    public List<String> getItems() { return items; }         // hands it back
}

List<String> in = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A"));
Order o = new Order(in);
in.add("HACK");          // mutates the order from outside
o.getItems().add("X");   // and from the getter — not immutable!
```

```java
public final class Order {
    private final List<String> items;
    public Order(List<String> items) {
        this.items = List.copyOf(items); // copy on input
    }
    public List<String> getItems() {
        return items; // List.copyOf returns an unmodifiable list
    }
}

List<String> in = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A"));
Order o = new Order(in);
in.add("HACK");                       // does NOT affect the order
// o.getItems().add("X");             // throws UnsupportedOperationException
```
*A Java `record` gives you final fields and no setters for free, but you still copy mutable members yourself.*

```java
interface PaymentProcessor {     // abstraction: the 'what'
    void process(Order order);
}

void checkout(Order o, PaymentProcessor p) {
    p.process(o); // caller has no idea if it's card, PayPal, UPI
}
```

```java
class CreditCardProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { // the 'how'
    private String cardNumber; // encapsulated: hidden, never exposed raw

    public void setCardNumber(String n) {
        if (n == null || n.length() != 16)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid card");
        this.cardNumber = n;
    }

    public void process(Order order) { /* charge using cardNumber */ }
}
```
*Abstraction defines the contract; encapsulation protects the data inside each implementation — they cooperate.*
